ork out the $x$-values using the formula $x=2t-1.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-7$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| $y$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=2-{t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-7$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-7$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055537/assets/5cd074670c05cea0c68db75c7e1a57309c017c97556e976f2db4c3bf1b358a85.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbf0ad0-a7c5-5c0f-a06c-9210fdc244ef" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ff75df2-f0a4-55fa-8aec-49f03ba3a757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332618} {:answer/id #uuid "fe32a594-fa7e-53ed-be6e-d2c71487faa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 1,\\,y\\le 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332619} {:answer/id #uuid "ad344e27-0877-50d0-8ff6-0df08263f401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -1,\\,y\\ge -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332620} {:answer/id #uuid "8164f275-97d2-5ddf-ac57-99c4d864bd15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -1,\\,y\\ge -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332621} {:answer/id #uuid "9687189a-1ca5-51ac-ae06-9c82b94d3337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1,\\,y\\le 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff75df2-f0a4-55fa-8aec-49f03ba3a757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332618} :answer-field/id #uuid "64ba1df4-fc8c-5af2-9275-b084a06a11ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7aeedf57-fc96-5a25-8888-075603ac5042" :question/math-academy-id "q-73623" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t-2}+1,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{3}+2,\\,t\\ge 2,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $\\sqrt{t-2}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\ge 2.$ Consequently, $x=\\sqrt{t-2}+1\\ge 1.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that ${t}^{3}\\ge 8$ for all $t\\ge 2.$ Consequently, $y={t}^{3}+2\\ge 8+2=10.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 10.$"}]}
